INT. HOLLOWAY & SONS FUNERAL HOME - BACK ROOM - DAY

Beige carpet. A box of tissues on every surface. A brass urn on a folding table between three siblings.

PRIYA SHENOY, 40, sensible blazer, stirs coffee. LEOPOLD "POLLY" SHENOY, 35, rumpled suit, reads the ceiling. DEV SHENOY, 28, sunglasses indoors, bounces a knee.

PRIYA
Dad would've wanted the lake.

POLLY
Dad hated the lake.

DEV
Dad hated everything with wet feet.

PRIYA
So we scatter at the lake anyway.

Polly nods. Dev nods. Priya sips. Nobody looks at the urn.

POLLY
Who's holding it in the car?

DEV
Not me.

PRIYA
Not me.

POLLY
Fine. Me.

He picks up the urn. Weighs it in both hands. Frowns slightly. Sets it down again. Picks it up again.

PRIYA
Polly.

POLLY
It's fine.

DEV
What's fine?

POLLY
It's fine. Probably fine.

PRIYA
Open it.

DEV
Don't open it.

POLLY
I'm opening it.

PRIYA
Polly, we are not opening our dead father in a funeral home.

POLLY
Dev, shut the door.

DEV
It's shut.

Polly twists the lid. A pale powder puffs. He sniffs. Dips a finger. Tastes.

All three freeze. Priya sets her coffee down very carefully.

POLLY
(quiet)
Buttermilk.

DEV
What?

POLLY
Pancake mix.

PRIYA
Dad's pancake mix is at the lake house.

DEV
Then where's Dad?

They look at a second brass urn on the shelf behind them. Same size. Same dent.

The three scatter toward the shelf, bumping shoulders, nobody speaking.
